# The plain-FP8 profile: the served configuration with the NVFP4 overlays off.
#
# Every projection is the checkpoint's own FP8Block (e4m3 codes, one bf16 scale per 128x128 block)
# and the head is the checkpoint's bf16 lm_head. Everything else is ops/serve.env as served: the
# drafters, the tree router, the verify graph, the commit fold, the fused GDN paths. The flags that
# only act on NVFP4 weights (QWEN38_NVFP4_SKINNY, _SRUN, _PREFILL_V2, the skinny tile tables) find no
# NVFP4 weight and do nothing; the drafter-side NVFP4 flags (QWEN38_DRAFT_NVFP4, _DRAFT_FC_NVFP4)
# act on the drafters' own weights and stay, because a drafter only proposes.
#
# StairCut's verify prices come with serve.env: ops/stair-tables-nvfp4.json, measured on the full-NVFP4
# profile. The router corrects their level online (its per-class block factor, engine/lenrouter.py),
# but the staircase's shape for this profile is not measured, and neither is its speed on this release.
#
# Use it where a tool takes a profile: ops/gate.sh --profile ops/serve-fp8.env, row3 with
# --nvfp4 "" --head "", the engine tools with QWEN38_NVFP4= QWEN38_FP8_HEAD= exported. docs/operations.md,
# "Profiles".
. "$(dirname "${BASH_SOURCE[0]}")/serve.env"
NV=
HEAD=
